# What silence looks like.
#
# No rt.json, no updates.xml, no weather.json, no pins. Every live input this
# app has is absent at once, which is the state it spends most of its life one
# network failure away from.
#
# This is the fixture that matters most to a second implementation. A broken
# realtime parse returns no arrivals, every row reads `sched`, and that looks
# exactly like a quiet Sunday. A detour feed whose tag convention changed
# reports no detours, which looks like a week with none. Both failures are
# invisible unless something writes down what "nothing" is supposed to draw.
#
# So: no glyph and no temperature on the rule, no warning on any screen, and
# `sched` on every departure of a bus route that does have realtime in the
# `drilldown` fixture. Anything else here is an implementation inventing data.
cache ../cache.db
date 2026-09-11
time 08:00
offset -04:00
size 100 14

# Bus -> 44 -> a direction -> a stop -> its board.
#
# Bus is row 0 here. It is row 1 in `drilldown`, where a pin sits above the
# modes. A script that hardcoded the row would walk into rail and still print
# frames that diff cleanly, so the two fixtures do not share their steps.
enter
type 44
enter

# The direction is named. A bare enter takes whatever the slice sorts first.
type hurdman
enter
enter

# And back out, so the route and direction screens are drawn with no detour
# on them rather than only walked through. Four levels down, four back up:
# board, stops, directions, routes.
esc
esc
esc
esc

# Then the same stop `drilldown` opens: pole 1869, TRANSITWAY / TERMINAL, which
# is the one stop this suite's rt.json carries arrivals for. There it reads
# `4 late`. Here it must read `sched` on every row, and that is the assertion
# this fixture exists to make -- the same stop, the same minute, the same
# timetable, and the only difference is a file that is not there.
type 1869
enter
